Context: this is part of Grothendieck's 1980s work, after the Longue Marche, on the 'loop equation'. He studies a profinite group G^+ (fraktur G, hat G^+ in the transcription). It is topologically generated by elements rho and sigma, plus a cusp loop epsilon_0, much like a profinite modular or triangle group. It also carries a distinguished normal subgroup G^natural, a kind of level structure. The object of study is the group Z_{rho,sigma} of quadruples (u, mu, eps_2, eps_3) such that:
- u is in Aut(G^+) and mu is in hat Z^*;
- eps_2, eps_3 are in mu_2 = {+1, -1};
- u(rho) is G^natural-conjugate to rho^{eps}, and u(sigma) is G^natural-conjugate to sigma^{eps};
- u(epsilon_0) = epsilon_0^mu;
- (condition (4)d) u stabilises G^natural.
The previous page divides Z by L_0^G, the inner automorphisms by powers of epsilon_0 that lie in Z. This gives Gamma_{rho,sigma} with characters eps_2, eps_3 : Gamma -> mu and chi : Gamma -> hat Z^*. The result is a GT-like group: automorphisms preserving the 'inertia' conjugacy classes, normalised at the cusp.

On this page:
(24) He sets Gamma', Gamma'', Gamma''' to be the kernels of eps_3, eps_2 and eps_2*eps_3. Gamma^0 is the common pairwise intersection. Z', Z'', Z''', Z^0 are their preimages in Z. These have index 1 or 2, and Gamma^0, Z^0 have index at most 4.
Remark 1: Z^0 is described as the set of pairs (u, mu) with u(rho) G^natural-conjugate to rho, u(sigma) G^natural-conjugate to sigma, and u(epsilon_0) = epsilon_0^mu. The claim is that stability of G^natural is then automatic. Indeed u(rho) is congruent to rho and u(sigma) to sigma mod G^natural, hence u(x) is congruent to x for all x (25bis). So u acts trivially on G^+/G^natural.
A marginal addition gives the analogous description of Z''' with a common sign eps. A second marginal note ('c'est mauvais ...') flags that (4)d) alone does not force trivial action on G^+/G^natural. So the general set-up must keep d) as a separate axiom.
Remark 2: the distinguished element tau_Z = (tau, -1, -1, -1) is the analogue of complex conjugation, inverting all the characters.
Construction, 'as on p. 577': Z maps to Aut(G^+). This gives semidirect products So G = Z ⋉ G^+, containing So G^natural = Z ⋉ G^natural (27). The anti-diagonal homomorphism l -> i_{L0,Z}(l)·l^{-1} embeds L_0^natural into So G^natural (28). Its image is normal, and the next page takes the quotient to obtain G_{rho,sigma}, an extension of Gamma_{rho,sigma} by G^+. This is the standard way to build a 'Galois-type' extension, i.e. an arithmetic-fundamental-group analogue, out of an automorphism group normalised at a tangential base point.
